        <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-extrabold text-zinc-950 dark:text-white tracking-tight">
          2. Stochastic Resonance: Why Brown Noise Calms the ADHD Brain
        </h2>
        <p>
          Attention-Deficit/Hyperactivity Disorder (ADHD) is neurochemically characterized by dysregulated dopamine transmission in the prefrontal cortex and striatal pathways. According to the <strong>Moderate Brain Arousal (MBA) model</strong> formulated by neuroscientists Sikström and Söderlund (2007), individuals with low basal dopamine levels suffer from internal neural "signal-to-noise" deficiency, leading to restlessness and executive function failure.
        </p>
        <p>
          When exposed to continuous low-frequency acoustic noise (specifically Brown noise), the brain undergoes <strong>Stochastic Resonance (SR)</strong>. Stochastic resonance is a non-linear biophysical phenomenon where optimal levels of background white/brown noise amplify sub-threshold neuronal signals, pushing them over the activation potential threshold without introducing cognitive distraction.
        </p>
        <p>
          In clinical trials, ADHD participants exposed to calibrated Brown and Pink noise demonstrated statistically significant improvements in working memory recall, reading comprehension, and task persistence compared to silent control conditions. The deep acoustic blanket occupies peripheral sensory channels, preventing the hyperactive thalamus from triggering involuntary attention shifts.
        </p>
      </section>

