        <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-extrabold text-zinc-950 dark:text-white tracking-tight">
          3. Tinnitus Management: Acoustic Masking & Residual Inhibition
        </h2>
        <p>
          Subjective tinnitus is frequently a symptom of cochlear hair cell damage or auditory deafferentation. When the brainstem ceases to receive normal baseline frequency inputs from the inner ear, the primary auditory cortex (A1) increases its central gain, generating phantom auditory percepts (ringing, buzzing, or hissing).
        </p>
        <p>
          Procedural acoustic soundscapes deliver dual clinical benefits for tinnitus sufferers:
        </p>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2 text-zinc-600 dark:text-zinc-300">
          <li>
            <strong>Complete Acoustic Masking:</strong> Broad-spectrum Pink and Brown noise elevates the ambient acoustic threshold above the loudness level of the phantom tinnitus frequency, providing immediate psychological relief.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Residual Inhibition & Habituation:</strong> Extended exposure to continuous non-repetitive noise reduces the central gain hyperactivity of the auditory cortex. After turning the noise off, patients often experience minutes of temporary reduction or complete absence of the phantom ringing.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

